2 TITL William Albertson, Quaker Colonist
1 _OID f4667b96-c67e-4567-be12-5f03273e28cc
1 DATE 2 May 1682
1 PLAC Newton Township, Gloucester, New Jersey, USA
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;A small group of Quaker trustees, one of them being William Penn, engineered  the division of Byllynge&amp;rsquo;s property into 90 shares, and anyone who was interested  in est
2 CONC ablishing a Quaker Colony could buy them.&amp;nbsp; Divided into tenths,  the first and second tenth portions became Burlington County and the third  and fourth tenth became Gloucester County.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The land that today comprises Magnol
2 CONC ia was situated in the third tenth portion of Gloucester County in Western New Jersey.&amp;nbsp; It was inhabited  by the friendly tribe of Lenni-Lenape Indians and was rich in woodland, flowing  streams and lakes with abundant supplies of fish.&am
2 CONC p;nbsp; The Quakers who later  settled here made a modest living off the timer, fruits, dairy products, vegetables and live stock which were indigenous to this climate and soil. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;       &lt;font face="verdana,geneva
2 CONC " size="2"&gt;On May 2, 1682, William Albertson, Sr. acquired a tract of this land in Newton Township and settled there.&amp;nbsp; Subsequently, he purchased several  more tracts of land, moved to Pennsylvania, and gave this land to his son  Willia
2 CONC m.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;p&gt;  &lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &amp;nbsp;In 1685, William, Sr. was a member of the Colonial Legislature and  also held other minor County and Township offices.&amp;nbsp; He reared six children  an
2 CONC d died in &lt;strong&gt;1709&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp; His son &lt;strong&gt;William&lt;/strong&gt; remained on the homestead until his  death in &lt;strong&gt;1720&lt;/strong&gt;, when this tract of land passed on to four generations of William  A
2 CONC lbertsons.&amp;nbsp; &amp;ldquo;Part of this land is the area we now know as Magnolia.&amp;rdquo;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &amp;nbsp;&amp;ldquo;On December 17, 1709, one of the tracts, located in Gloucester Township  and bounded on the south side by Ot
2 CONC ter Branch, became the legacy to &lt;strong&gt;Josiah  Albertson&lt;/strong&gt;, who then settled there.&amp;rdquo;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Josiah&lt;/strong&gt; married &lt;strong&gt;Ann Austin&lt;/strong&gt; of Evesham, Burli
2 CONC ngton County, in 1727.&amp;nbsp;  They had eight daughters and one son.&amp;nbsp; He plied his trade as a shoemaker,  but at the same time increased his land holdings to twice the size of the  original acreage, on which he established grist and sa
2 CONC w mills and thrived  on farming and lumbering.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  In 1743, &lt;strong&gt;Josiah&lt;/strong&gt; built a large brick house, possibly on the site of his log  cabin.&amp;nbsp; Today it is a three-story structure with twenty rooms.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;  Still intact are the original yellow pine floors and woodwork, built-in cupboards  with brass hardware dating back to 1869 and four working fireplaces.&amp;nbsp;  The house was added to, and is still standing and occupied.&amp;nbsp; Carve
2 CONC d on the cornerstone on the west wall of this remarkable house is the phrase, &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;Built by Josiah Albertson 1743, rebuilt by Chalkley Albertson 1865.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; It is an impressive looking mansion locat
2 CONC ed several hundred yards off the south side of Evesham Road, Gloucester Township, just across the Magnolia Borough line.&amp;nbsp; It is the birthplace of this immediate area.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  Like his father, &lt;strong&gt;Josiah Albertson &lt
2 CONC ;/strong&gt;was active in civic affairs.&amp;nbsp; In those days, civic meetings were held in March of each year at different homes in the area.&amp;nbsp; Minutes of Old Gloucester Township dated 1747 reveal that  one of the annual meetings was hel
2 CONC d in Josiah&amp;rsquo;s home on Evesham Road, at which time he was elected town clerk for that year.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  At the time of Josiah&amp;rsquo;s death, &lt;strong&gt;Chalkley Albertson,&lt;/strong&gt; who was born on this estate and wa
2 CONC s in direct line, also owned a portion of this estate, purchased the rest and before long became a successful farmer.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;--- ----- ---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;The New Jersey Albertson
2 CONC s were Quakers. He was one of the first settlers in Newton, Gloucester Co. On &lt;strong&gt;May 2, 1682,&lt;/strong&gt; he located on a tract there and operated a sawmill. He may have been from New Amsterdam or Long Island. Later, in &lt;strong&gt;
2 CONC 1692&lt;/strong&gt;, he moved across the Delaware River to &lt;strong&gt;Byberry Twp&lt;/strong&gt;., Philadlephia Co.. In &lt;strong&gt;1698&lt;/strong&gt;, he conveyed the estate to his &lt;strong&gt;son William&lt;/strong&gt;. He was a member o
2 CONC f the colonial legislature in &lt;strong&gt;1685&lt;/strong&gt;, and held other posts.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;--- ----- ---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;From &amp;quot;Sketches of the First Emigrant Settlers
2 CONC , Newton Twp.&amp;quot; by John Clement (1877) pp. 101-108;&lt;br&gt;Phila Liber M 75; Magazine of American Genealogy, 1929:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;On &lt;strong&gt;May 2, 1682&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;
2 CONC strong&gt;William Albertson&lt;/strong&gt; located a tract of land in Newton township, between the south and the middle branch of the creek that bears that name, and settled thereon. &lt;strong&gt;[4-5] &lt;/strong&gt;(Today the site is in Camden
2 CONC , near the Walt Whitman bridge, just across the creek from Gloucester City) It does not appear whence he came, but the probability is that he was of Dutch extraction, as before named, and that his parents were among the Hollanders of New York. Th
2 CONC e house which he built--no doubt, a small one--stood by the middle branch, and nearly fronting the little settlement called Newton; but in a few years it entirely disappeared. He&lt;br&gt;  was a married man with a family when he came there; shortl
2 CONC y after he removed to &lt;strong&gt;Byberry&lt;/strong&gt;, Pennsylvania, and gave the possession of the estate to his son William. This occurred before 1692, for, in that year, he purchased a tract of land in the town bounds of &lt;strong&gt;Glouc
2 CONC ester&lt;/strong&gt;, the deed for which names him as then a resident of the place above mentioned. &lt;strong&gt;[6]&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  A portion of this same land was still in possession of a lineal descendent, &lt;strong&gt;Joh
2 CONC n Jarrett Albertson&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;strong&gt;1923&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  Upon the setting apart of a lot of land at Newton whereon to build a meeting house, he was one of the persons who accepted the trust therefor, and no doub
2 CONC t took an active part in the erection of that place of worship.&lt;strong&gt;[7]&lt;/strong&gt; This trust was continued until 1708, when other and younger men were called to occupy the same position.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  He made several location
2 CONC s and purchases of land, while a resident here; but his removal so soon from this neighborhood leaves but little of his history among us, yet, so far as his record goes, he was a person much respected in his day and generation. In 1685, he was retu
2 CONC rned as a member of the 4 Lib. T, 355, O. S. G. Colonial Legislature; he also held other minor county and township offices during his settlement here. &lt;strong&gt;[8]&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  His children were: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; William&lt;/strong&gt;, who married &lt;strong&gt;Esther Willis&lt;/strong&gt;, daughter of &lt;strong&gt;Henry &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Mary&lt;/strong&gt;, of Westbury, Long Island, N. Y., in &lt;strong&gt;1695&lt;/strong&gt;
2 CONC ; &lt;strong&gt;[9] &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Abraham&lt;/strong&gt;, who married Hannah Medcalf; &lt;strong&gt;[10] &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Rebecca&lt;/strong&gt;, who married Joseph Satterthwaite
2 CONC ; &lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Ann&lt;/strong&gt;, who married &lt;strong&gt;Walter Forrest&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;[11]&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;John Kaighn&lt;/strong&gt;; &lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Cassandra&lt;/strong
2 CONC &gt;, who married &lt;strong&gt;Jarvis Stockdale&lt;/strong&gt;; &lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Benjamin&lt;/strong&gt;, who married &lt;strong&gt;Sarah Walton&lt;/strong&gt;; and&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; Josiah&lt;/strong&gt;, who mar
2 CONC ried &lt;strong&gt;Ann Austin&lt;/strong&gt; of Evesham, Burlington county, N. J.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  A &lt;strong&gt;weaver&lt;/strong&gt; by trade, at the time of his decease, he resided at &lt;strong&gt;Poquesin&lt;/strong&gt;, in Bucks county
2 CONC , Pennsylvania, where he owned grain and saw mills, and considerable other property. He died soon after the execution of his will (&lt;strong&gt;1709&lt;/strong&gt;), survived by his widow &lt;strong&gt;Hannah&lt;/strong&gt;, and by all his childre
2 CONC n except &lt;strong&gt;Ann&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;[12]&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  To his son &lt;strong&gt;William&lt;/strong&gt;, in the year &lt;strong&gt;1698,&lt;/strong&gt; he deeded the homestead property, whereon he remaine
2 CONC d until his decease in 1720. &lt;strong&gt;[13]&lt;/strong&gt; This was a valuable estate, and he improved it by enlarging and banking the meadow attached to the property, which, at that time, was the only soil from which hay and pasture were deriv
2 CONC ed. The artificial grasses now used upon the upland, had not then attracted the attention of agriculturists, for which reason the meadow and marsh lands along the streams commanded much the higher price, and were considered as a necessary appendag
2 CONC e to every farm. The meadow land on each of the branches of Newton creek, was, no doubt, the attraction that brought the settlers first to this place, and was, in fact, the only means they had for sustaining their cattle.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;  To av
2 CONC oid expense and to secure the land from the overflow of the tide,&lt;strong&gt; William Albertson &lt;/strong&gt;placed a dam across the south branch, and reclaimed much of the marsh above the same. In this dam there were tide gates, the constructi
2 CONC on and utility of which need not be explained here. &lt;strong&gt;[14]&lt;/strong&gt; These were kept in use until the dyke was put across the mouth of the creek, at the river, in 1786.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva
2 CONC " size="2"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Notes:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;font face="courier new,courier"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[8]&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Leaming &amp;amp; Spicer&amp;#39;s Laws.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;st
2 CONC rong&gt;[9]&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Friends&amp;#39; Records, Long Island.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;[10]&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;License Book, 25, Lib. No. 7, Salem Records, 1
2 CONC 56.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;[11]&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Lib. No. 6, Salem Records, 32.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;[12]&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Philadelphia Records. Lib. M
2 CONC , 75, O. S. G.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;[13]&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Lib. A, 104. Lib. G3, 139. Lib. No. 2, 139.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;[14]&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;font face="verdana,geneva"&gt;Alber
2 CONC tson Papers.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;                &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Reference&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" s
2 CONC ize="2" color="#0000ff"&gt;http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~jleatham/William_Albertson.html&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;by Jacky Leatham, 2003.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font face="verdana,geneva" size="2"&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
